Has anybody noticed that magic lamps never run out of oil, and therefore stay
permanently lit? If you apply a magic lamp, it turns on like a normal oil lamp
and lights up the room, and goes out if you drop it, like a normal lamp. But
it never runs out of lighting capability until you rub it and free the djinni,
after what it becomes a normal oil lamp that will run out of oil in time.
This feature makes it *almost* not worth rubbing the lamp to get a wish...
At any rate, if you know you have a cursed or uncursed magic lamp, you can
always use it as a source of light until you find a way to bless it... (a
cursed lamp is slightly harder to turn on, but it will if you apply it a
large enough number of times). I personally find it useful to walk around
with a lit lamp when exploring even normal dungeon levels, as corridors get
lit quite a ways around you, and exploring goes slightly faster (not to mention
the advantage of seeing this &%$!@# grey elf who's hiding in some corner of
a dark room shooting arrows at you...). The permanently-stay-lit feature can
be used to identify magic lamps: pick up a lamp and turn it on and walk around
with it for a long time. If the light goes out eventually, you can #name that
category of lamp "oil lamp", and any lamp you find later that is not named
"oil lamp" is a magic lamp.

Now, on the subject of identifying unknown wands, the spoilers to NH3.0 describe
describe a method where you first engrave with the wand, then zap it at
monsters, then at yourself, then at doors, and all this uses a lot of
charges. Here is my favorite method that is perfectly safe, and requires only
at most two charges in most cases (usually only one):
1) find a door (not a shop door!) and close it if opened
2) in front of it, drop a monster corpse (preferably an easy monster to kill)
and an object that will become different if cancelled (either a scroll that will
become blank, or a potion that will become water, or a blessed or cursed object
that will become uncursed, or an object with a bonus (e.g. weapon or armor)
that will become 0)
3) step back and zap the wand at the two objects and the door at the same time
(this is safe because rays don't bounce back from doors and can't hurt you).
Here are the effects you get for the various wands:
a) cancellation: the cancellable object has become cancelled (see 2) above)
(you have to name the wand, door not affected)
b) cold: "the door freezes and shatters" (wand automatically named, door 
destroyed)
c) create monster: monster is created next to you (wand automatically named,
door not affected)
d) death: "the door absorbs your bolt" (wand automatically named, door not
affected)
e) digging: a tunnel is created (wand automatically named, door disappears)
f) fire: "the door is consumed in flames" (wand automatically named, door
destroyed)
g) locking: "the door locks" if door was unlocked (wand automatically named)
no message, no apparent effect if door was already locked (you can zap an
unknown wand at first a locked, then an unlocked door to find out for sure)
h) light: "a lit field surrounds you" (wand automatically named, door not
affected)
i) lightning: "the door splinters" (wand automatically named, door destroyed)
j) magic missile: "the door absorbs your bolt" (wand automatically named, door
not affected)
k) make invisible: no message, no apparent effect; requires a second zap at
yourself if visible or some visible monster (then wand automatically named)
l) nothing: no message, no apparent effect (has to be named; if it is no
other wand, then it's a wand of nothing)
m) opening: "the door unlocks" if door was locked (wand automatically named)
no message, no apparent effect if door was already unlocked (you can zap an
unknown wand at first a locked, then an unlocked door to find out for sure)
n) polymorph: objects in front of door have been polymorphed (not that wand of
cancellation did not affect monster corpse, whereas wand of polymorph changes it
to some other kind of food) (wand has to be named, although if you drop a large
pile of stuff you might get a "shuddering vibrations" message and wand is
automatically named; door not affected)
o) probing: no message, no apparent effect; requires a second zap at yourself
(you get your status, wand automatically named)
p) secret door detection: no message; if there was a secret door in the room, 
would be automatically named; however, only non-directional wand that does
not get automatically identified. If you zap it, are not prompted for a
direction and it does not get automatically identified, then it's a wand
of secret door detection and you can name it yourself.
q) sleep: "the door absorbs your bolt" (automatically named, door not affected)
r) slow monster: no message, no apparent effect; zapping it at yourself can be
bad (only wand for which this is true with this method): if you were fast you 
lose your intrinsic speed, however if you were at normal speed you don't get
any message and no apparent effect. You can zap it at other monsters to see
if it slows them down. In any case, even if it makes you slow down, you have
to name it yourself.
s) speed monster: no message, no apparent effect; a second zap at yourself
will speed you up (wand automatically named)
t) striking: "the door crashes open" (automatically named, door destroyed)
u) teleportation: no messages, but objects disappear (have been teleported
elsewhere on the level) (you have to name the wand yourself, door not affected)
v) undead turning: corpse comes back to life (you have to name the wand, door
not affected)
w) wishing: you get a wish (wand automatically named)

